From: codepoet80 <jon.wise@jonandnic.com>
Date: Wed, 17 Sep 2026 17:57:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sched: park SysV IPC in the ANDROID_KABI reserved padding

LuneOS needs SysV IPC: libPmLogLib calls shmget()/shmat(), and without it
every PmLogCtl invocation blocks forever waiting for com.webos.pmlogd.
surface-manager.sh runs "PmLogCtl def surface-manager" on its first line, so
on the Pixel Tablet the compositor never started and the device sat on the
boot logo. The same failure is documented on MP01.

Enabling CONFIG_SYSVIPC the naive way is KMI poison: sysv_sem and sysv_shm
land in the middle of task_struct and every following member shifts, which
moves the genksyms CRC of every exported symbol whose prototype mentions a
task_struct. Under CONFIG_MODVERSIONS the stock vendor modules then refuse to
load, and losing them loses the Tier A property the GKI port rests on.

Park the two members in the reserved Android KABI padding instead. sysv_sem is
one pointer and fits a single slot via ANDROID_KABI_USE(6, ...); sysv_shm is a
list_head and no USE macro spans two slots, so it is written longhand across
slots 7 and 8 in the shape the macro would have produced. android_kabi.h
defines _ANDROID_KABI_REPLACE(_orig, _new) as _orig under __GENKSYMS__, so
genksyms still sees the untouched reserved u64s and the ABI it computes does
not move.

Verified on tangorpro against the stock cp3a.260905.009 vendor:

  - kmi-crc-check: 194/194 stock vendor_kernel_boot modules OK.
  - Module.symvers byte-identical to the same tree built with SYSVIPC off.
  - BTF comparison of 10,486 structs: only task_struct differs, and only by
    naming slots 6-8 - thread is still at offset 3664 and the size is still
    4800. A CRC check alone cannot show this, because the __GENKSYMS__ trick
    keeps CRCs stable by construction.
  - On device: /proc/sysvipc exists, PmLogCtl returns immediately, and the
    compositor comes up.

gs101 and gs201 share one KMI, so this covers bluejay and panther too.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [embedded specific]

The patch exists to keep a vendor's prebuilt modules loadable against a
rebuilt ACK. It depends on the Android KABI padding macros and on slots 6-8 of
task_struct being free in this tree, so it is specific to this KMI and has no
meaning upstream.
---
--- a/include/linux/sched.h
+++ b/include/linux/sched.h
@@ -1096,10 +1096,11 @@
 
 	struct nameidata		*nameidata;
 
-#ifdef CONFIG_SYSVIPC
-	struct sysv_sem			sysvsem;
-	struct sysv_shm			sysvshm;
-#endif
+	/*
+	 * LuneOS: sysvsem/sysvshm (CONFIG_SYSVIPC) would normally live here.
+	 * Inserting them mid-struct shifts every following member and breaks
+	 * the frozen KMI, so they are parked in ANDROID_KABI_USE(6..8) below.
+	 */
 #ifdef CONFIG_DETECT_HUNG_TASK
 	unsigned long			last_switch_count;
 	unsigned long			last_switch_time;
@@ -1553,9 +1554,19 @@
 	ANDROID_KABI_RESERVE(3);
 	ANDROID_KABI_RESERVE(4);
 	ANDROID_KABI_RESERVE(5);
-	ANDROID_KABI_RESERVE(6);
-	ANDROID_KABI_RESERVE(7);
-	ANDROID_KABI_RESERVE(8);
+	/*
+	 * LuneOS: SysV IPC in reserved padding. genksyms still sees the
+	 * reserved u64s, so CRCs and offsets match the SYSVIPC=n KMI.
+	 * sysv_sem is one pointer (one slot), sysv_shm a list_head (two).
+	 */
+#ifdef CONFIG_ANDROID_KABI_RESERVE
+	ANDROID_KABI_USE(6, struct sysv_sem sysvsem);
+	_ANDROID_KABI_REPLACE(_ANDROID_KABI_RESERVE(7); _ANDROID_KABI_RESERVE(8),
+			      struct sysv_shm sysvshm);
+#else
+	struct sysv_sem			sysvsem;
+	struct sysv_shm			sysvshm;
+#endif
 
 	/*
 	 * New fields for task_struct should be added above here, so that
@@ -1574,6 +1585,13 @@
 	 */
 };
 
+#if defined(CONFIG_SYSVIPC) && defined(CONFIG_ANDROID_KABI_RESERVE)
+static_assert(sizeof(struct sysv_sem) == sizeof(u64),
+	      "sysv_sem must occupy exactly one ANDROID_KABI reserved slot");
+static_assert(sizeof(struct sysv_shm) == 2 * sizeof(u64),
+	      "sysv_shm must occupy exactly two ANDROID_KABI reserved slots");
+#endif
+
 static inline struct pid *task_pid(struct task_struct *task)
 {
 	return task->thread_pid;
